<h2>How to compare agent security tools without marketing fog</h2>
<p>Teams evaluating agent security tooling in 2026 usually mix three layers: prompt-injection defenses (OWASP LLM01), tool-allowlist enforcement, and runtime monitoring. A useful comparison grid separates <em>pre-run policy</em>, <em>in-run controls</em>, and <em>post-run evidence</em>. Pre-run policy covers system prompts, allowed tools, and data classification. In-run controls cover sandboxes, rate limits, and human approval gates. Post-run evidence covers logs, red-team transcripts, and incident tickets. If a vendor collapses all three into one score, ask for artifact examples rather than accepting a single percentage.</p>
<p>Category:baseline checklists help more than feature matrices. For each candidate tool, record whether it (1) maps findings to a published framework such as OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications, (2) supports offline or air-gapped evaluation, (3) exports machine-readable results, and (4) states failure modes honestly. Prefer tools that fail closed when credentials are missing over tools that invent “green” results. That honesty rule matches how production incident reviews actually work.</p>
<p>Shared blog stubs across micro-SaaS fleets often under-serve readers because they stay at definition level. Expand the comparison with decision criteria: latency budget, false-positive tolerance, who owns remediations, and whether the tool can run without uploading proprietary prompts. Write down what you will <em>not</em> automate—destructive tool calls, production write access, and payment mutations should stay behind human approval even when scanners pass.</p>
<p>When you publish a comparison for your own product category, cite primary sources (OWASP, NIST AI RMF) and keep competitor names factual. Do not invent customer win rates. A durable comparison page is a checklist plus worked examples, not a leaderboard.</p>
<h2>Practical scoring rubric (copy into your sheet)</h2>
<ul>
<li>Policy clarity: can an engineer explain allow/deny rules in five minutes?</li>
<li>Evidence quality: do outputs include inputs, tool traces, and timestamps?</li>
<li>Failure behavior: 503/429/502 style honesty when upstream AI is down.</li>
<li>Operator load: hours per week to maintain deny lists and fixtures.</li>
<li>Fit: matches your agent architecture (tool-calling vs retrieval-only).</li>
</ul>
<p>Repeat the rubric quarterly. Agent frameworks change faster than annual vendor reviews. Keep a short “What this comparison is NOT” note on every page: not a certification, not a penetration test, and not a guarantee that agents will never abuse tools.</p>

